course: dictionary
lesson: 4896
title: Il, a small word with big jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Il» is not an English word, but you may spot it in French and Italian. In French it usually means «he» or «it». In «il mange», it means «he eats», and the verb tells you who is doing the eating.
explain: Sometimes French uses «il» with no real person. «Il pleut» means «it is raining». The «il» is only a placeholder, a bit like the «it» in the English sentence «it is cold».
explain: The same spelling means something different in Italian. «Il gelato» means «the ice cream», so here «il» means «the». To know which language you are reading, look at the words around it.
fact: «Il» is not an English word.
fact: In French, «il» means «he» or «it».
fact: «Il pleut» means «it is raining».
fact: In Italian, «il» means «the», as in «il gelato», the ice cream.
fact: To an English ear, French «il» sounds like «eel».
exercise: In French, «il mange» means he eats. What does «il» mean here? | Please | *He | The | Ice cream
exercise: In «il pleut», what does «il» stand for? | The | *No real person, just a placeholder for it | Ice | Rain
exercise: In Italian, «il gelato» means the ice cream. What does «il» mean there? | Please | Eel | He | *The
test: Which phrase means «it is raining»? | Il mange | *Il pleut | Il gelato | Il y a
test: To an English ear, French «il» sounds like which English word? | *Eel | Ill | Fill | Hill
exam: The phrase «il gelato» comes from which language, and what does «il» mean there? | French, meaning please | *Italian, meaning the | French, meaning he | English, meaning it
